  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,5]=[0,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $1$ | $4$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274355/assets/3421933d57ba9341f1743ecc47507966f91d1d7c134d194cbe5bc5d9ef51fadd.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{3+1}{2}\\cdot (2-0)+\\frac{1+4}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+\\frac{15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
